# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings are pinned to LF for everything that is read by something other
# than Visual Studio.  This matters on a checkout that is opened on both Windows
# and Linux, because Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true and would
# otherwise check these files out with CRLF:
#
#   *.sh  a CRLF shebang line is not a shebang. bash reports the interpreter as
#         "/bin/bash\r" and the script fails with "$'\r': command not found",
#         which reads like a typo in the script rather than a checkout problem.
#   *.py  a CRLF file runs fine, but a CRLF *shebang* in write_mcp_config.py
#         and smoke_test.py does not, for the same reason as *.sh.
#   *.cmd tolerates either, so it is left at whatever git decides.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# C++ and shaders: normalise on checkout. A CRLF in a GLSL file would be a
# comment-terminator hazard rather than a syntax error, so it is worth pinning.
*.cpp text eol=lf
*.h text eol=lf
*.vert text eol=lf
*.frag text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never rewrites it.
*.png binary
*.ppm binary
*.spv binary
